(EXP-26: LANGUAGE.md:1854 offers
 `{fibonacci of n subtract 1} add {fibonacci of n subtract 2}` as an example of
 a braced arithmetic subexpression. It parses, but NOT as the fibonacci
 recurrence: `of` binds only the next primary, so the braces contain
 `{fibonacci of n} subtract 1`, the argument never shrinks, and the call
 recurses until the runtime stops it. See Discrepancy 1.
 What this probe records is that the runtime catches it: the depth guard
 fires and the program exits 1 with a diagnostic, rather than smashing the
 stack. That is the memory-safety half of the row and it holds.
 The working spelling is `{fibonacci of {n subtract 1}}` - see D1.vox.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox EXP-26.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   Error: stack overflow (recursion depth exceeded)
   exit 1
)
To fibonacci with a number called n.
  If n is less than 2 then, Return a number, n.
  Return a number, {fibonacci of n subtract 1} add {fibonacci of n subtract 2}.

Print fibonacci of 10.
